---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CaptureForm from "../../components/forms/capture-form.astro";

const seo = {
  page_description: "Detailed reference on PDF Security, Encryption standards (RC4, AES), and passwords.",
};
---
<Layout title="PDF Security & Encryption Standards" seo={seo}>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-8 py-16 prose">
    <h1>PDF Security & Encryption Standards</h1>
    <p>PDF security is built directly into the file format specifications (see <a href="/reference/topic-1">ISO 32000-1</a>). It defines how documents are encrypted and what permissions are granted to the user.</p>

    <h2>Passwords vs. Permissions</h2>
    <p>The PDF specification differentiates between two types of passwords:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>User Password (Document Open Password):</strong> Encrypts the file. You cannot open or read the PDF without it.</li>
      <li><strong>Owner Password (Permissions Password):</strong> Does not prevent opening, but restricts actions like printing, copying text, or <a href="/tools/annotate-pdf">annotating the PDF</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Encryption Algorithms History</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>PDF Version</th>
          <th>Encryption Standard</th>
          <th>Security Status (Current)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>PDF 1.1 to 1.3</td>
          <td>40-bit RC4</td>
          <td>Severely Compromised (Do not use)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>PDF 1.4</td>
          <td>128-bit RC4</td>
          <td>Weak</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>PDF 1.6</td>
          <td>128-bit AES</td>
          <td>Acceptable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>PDF 1.7 Extension 3+</td>
          <td>256-bit AES</td>
          <td><strong>Strong (Recommended)</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Implications for Annotations</h2>
    <p>If an Owner Password is set with restrictions against modifications, standard PDF readers will disable annotation tools. However, it's a known industry secret that "permissions" encryption is largely enforced by the "honor system" of the viewer software, unlike "open" encryption which actually scrambles the data.</p>

    <p>If you need to archive a secure document for long-term storage, consider the <a href="/glossary/term-3">PDF/A Standard</a> which has specific rules regarding encryption.</p>

    <div class="mt-12 not-prose p-6 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold mb-4">Did this technical reference help?</h3>
      <CaptureForm formLabel="feedback" submitText="Send Feedback" />
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
